import pathlib,json,hashlib,shutil,subprocess,csv
root=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4];out=root/'results/real-app/multi-bundler-robustness-v1';rows=json.load(open(out/'mutation-probes.json'))['results'];scale=json.load(open(out/'synthetic-scale.json'))['rows'];real=json.load(open(root/'results/real-app/multi-bundler-v1/aggregate.json'))
assert len(rows)==38 and all('error'not in x for x in rows);assert sum(x['pass']for x in rows)==28;assert all(x['observedCallEdges']==x['expectedCallEdges']for x in scale)
files=['analyzer.mjs','module-graph.mjs','analyze.mjs','extract.mjs','propagate.mjs'];sha=lambda p:hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest();provenance={p:sha(root/'scripts/real-app/multi-bundler'/p)for p in files};provenance['node']=subprocess.check_output(['node','--version'],text=True).strip();provenance['esbuild']=subprocess.check_output(['node','--input-type=module','-e',"import {version} from 'esbuild';console.log(version)"],text=True).strip()
for subject in real['repos']:
 d=json.load(open(root/'results/real-app/multi-bundler-v1'/subject['repo'].replace('/','--')/'summary.json'));assert d['analysis']['source']['analyzerSha256']==provenance['analyzer.mjs'];assert d['analysis']['bundle']['moduleGraphSha256']==provenance['module-graph.mjs']
(out/'provenance.json').write_text(json.dumps(provenance,indent=2)+'\n')
with open(out/'mutation-probes.csv','w',newline='')as f:
 w=csv.DictWriter(f,fieldnames=['family','name','group','expected','observed','pass']);w.writeheader();w.writerows({k:r[k]for k in w.fieldnames}for r in rows)
lines=['# Generalization and scalability audit of the current multi-bundler analyzer','','## Assessment','','The shared analysis design is portable and has demonstrated useful scale, but the current implementation is not ready for a claim that it robustly handles most repositories using these bundlers. Ordinary application edits do not inherently invalidate the design; however, ordinary new dataflow, equivalent emitted syntax, supported bundler configuration changes, or missing runtime context can reduce coverage. One targeted case produces a spurious cross-loader edge. These are observed limits of the unchanged implementation, not newly repaired behavior.','','Three questions must be kept separate: computation cost, recognition/relationship coverage, and anchor-based recovery after a build changes. Passing one does not prove the other two.','','## Measured computation cost','','Existing evidence is eight convenience-selected, pinned non-esbuild applications: one Rspack, three Rolldown-backed builds, three Turbopack builds and one Parcel extension. There are 69 selected repo/library cases, 62 with supported targets. This is not a random or representative sample of the ecosystem. The selected oracle is not a complete inventory of all functions or libraries in those applications.','','The largest prior subject, ZyPlayer, supplied 4,265 source files and 102 prepared chunks, with approximately 45.9 MB source JavaScript and 51.6 MB bundled JavaScript. Extraction took 25.6 seconds on the source side and 18.9 seconds on the bundle side. Completion RSS was approximately 2.6 GiB and 3.3 GiB respectively; this was not sampled peak memory, and the prepared input omitted some emitted chunks. Its runtime-helper diagnostic is separate and does not establish full-build performance.','','Fresh-process synthetic chains were generated at 500, 2,000 and 8,000 modules for each backend. Every expected direct-call edge was found. Each case used one trial; times include parsing, constraint compilation, solving and module-graph extraction. RSS is completion RSS, not peak. These simple chains exercise module/export resolution, not realistic worst-case JavaScript.','','| Bundler | 500 modules: seconds / MiB | 2,000 modules: seconds / MiB | 8,000 modules: seconds / MiB |','|---|---:|---:|---:|']
for family in ['rspack','rolldown','turbopack','parcel']:
 xs=[r for r in scale if r['family']==family];lines.append('| '+family+' | '+' | '.join(f"{r['elapsedMs']/1000:.3f} / {r['rssEndMiB']}"for r in xs)+' |')
lines+=['','The worklist schedules constraints from changed slots and retains one input AST at a time. Whole-build constraint/value state is still retained. Value sets are capped at 64; overflow causes abstention. An event budget of 50 million fails the analysis. Neither bound proves favorable worst-case complexity.','','A concrete avoidable scaling issue remains: `module-graph.mjs` filters the complete module list for every lazy CommonJS require dependency. This can cost O(L × M), where L is the number of lazy-require references and M is the number of module records. A module-key index would remove that repeated scan. Value unions and property propagation can also increase with ambiguity. The synthetic numbers alone do not support a linear-time claim.','','## Changes tested without modifying the analyzer','','Thirty-eight targeted probes were run: 28 satisfied their expected edge behavior, and 10 exposed missing or incorrect relationships. They were chosen to test mechanisms and limits, not sampled to estimate repository failure rates. These are small static relationship probes, not freshly rebuilt production applications or model-recovery experiments. The esbuild transform is a semantics-preserving post-minification of fixtures, not evidence about the native minifier output of every backend.','','| Change | Observation |','|---|---|','| Local variable/function renaming | Passed on all four fixture families |','| Consistent export-property renaming | Passed on all four fixture families |','| Comments/whitespace, unrelated code and lexical shadowing | Passed on all four fixture families |','| Module IDs changed to strings and require aliases | Passed on Rspack fixtures |','| Native ESM chunk hashes changed and file order reversed | Passed with consistent imports and complete supplied context |','| Runtime helper moved to a supplied separate chunk | Passed on Rolldown fixture |','| esbuild minification | Passed on Rolldown, Turbopack and Parcel fixtures; failed on the Rspack fixture |','| Rspack custom chunk-loading global | Failed to recognize the modules |','| Rspack bracket-to-dot global property syntax | Failed to recognize the modules |','| Turbopack factory moved into a variable | Missed the intended relationship |','| Parcel registration method assigned to an alias | Missed registrations and intended relationship |','| Two independent Parcel loader namespaces | Produced an incorrect cross-loader call relationship |','| Missing external runtime helper | Missed the intended relationship |','| A function passed through an ordinary identity function | Missed the resulting indirect call |','| Constant computed property expression, `ns["g"+"o"]` | Missed the target; constant folding is absent here |','| Literal `await import("./lib.js")` | Missed namespace-to-target flow despite the supplied file |','','The Rspack minifier probe reduces bracket access to dot access:','','```js','// Recognized','self["rspackChunkdemo"]','// Not recognized by the current registration recognizer','self.rspackChunkdemo','```','','The implementation uses a quoted-string prefix regex for the container. Both forms can express the same property access, but the recognizer has not normalized their AST representations. A custom name such as `myApplicationChunks` also fails even in bracket form. This is a real configuration surface: [Rspack output documentation](https://rspack.rs/config/output#outputchunkloadingglobal) permits custom chunk globals and documents array-push, CommonJS and module chunk formats. Only tested format families should be claimed.','','The Parcel adapter derives a namespace from the first `parcelRequire*` text occurrence in a file and applies it to all `.register` calls. In the negative fixture, only loader A defines a requested module ID; a consumer using independent loader B incorrectly resolves A\'s export. This is a correctness defect in runtime binding attribution. Separate manifests are currently required for distinct execution environments, but the analyzer must also preserve different loader bindings within a supplied file.','','Rolldown helper summaries use AST features and parameter roles rather than minified local helper names, which makes renaming portable. However, helpers must be supplied, summary heuristics accept only certain shapes and sizes, and unknown ordinary function-return flow is not modeled. Turbopack recognizes tested production push/inline-factory formats and context methods; hoisted factory references fail. Parcel recognizes direct register calls plus flattened lexical code; registration aliases fail. Development, HMR, other output modes and arbitrary plugin rewrites are not established by the current corpus. [Rolldown output formats](https://rolldown.rs/reference/OutputOptions.format), [Parcel target options](https://parceljs.org/features/targets/).','','## What an application edit changes','','A normal edit does not require matching a fixed list of repository-specific variable names. The generic analyzer uses the new code, lexical scopes, static imports, factory roles and allocation sites. But all graph inventories, spans, hashes and initial seeds must be recomputed for the rebuilt artifact; old anchors or offsets cannot be carried forward blindly.','','An application edit can still change recovery even if module recognition succeeds:','','- Adding a higher-order call or computed import/property flow can introduce a relationship the current value analysis misses. Three direct flow probes demonstrate this without changing bundler protocols.','- Adding a structurally similar function can destroy the uniqueness of an existing POG seed. The current oracle simulation holds seeds fixed within one build; it does not establish seed stability across rebuilt applications.','- A dependency change, tree shaking, inlining or module concatenation can change which source functions survive as separately supported bundled functions. This requires a new oracle population, not reuse of the old denominator.','- A new split point can move a helper or useful connecting function into a context-only chunk. The current generic CLI consumes the supplied manifest; it does not recursively discover all imports. The frozen experiment runner supplies prepared target-containing chunks only.','- The 18,000-character promotion limit, ambiguity policy and source parser coverage can further change the set of promotable functions.','','The prior icon diagnostic is a concrete input-context counterexample: adding only its actual imported runtime helper, with no new matchable functions, changed connectivity from 1,530/41,489 to 41,489/41,489. It demonstrates why target population and analysis-context population must be independent. It does not prove 100% recovery for arbitrary future builds.','','## Improvements needed before a broad robustness claim','','1. Collect the static dependency closure of entry/selected chunks, including runtime-only files, and partition browser, worker, server and independent loader environments. Report unresolved imports and unsupported registry operations as coverage failures. Convergence is not completeness.','2. Normalize syntax and resolve runtime/loader bindings structurally: bracket/dot access, factory aliases, registration aliases, custom globals and distinct registries. Fix the observed Parcel cross-loader defect before relying on new connectivity gains.','3. Add bounded ordinary argument/return propagation, constant-string evaluation and literal dynamic-import namespace flow. Preserve explicit abstention for unresolved or ambiguous cases.','4. Index lazy module records by key, measure peak memory and profile complete artifact closures; add caching or staged processing where measured bottlenecks justify it.','5. Build a real rebuild matrix over held-out applications and multiple supported versions/modes. Vary minification, scope hoisting/concatenation, chunk splitting/runtime extraction, custom globals, output format and ordinary application refactorings. Rebuild source-map oracles and seeds for each build; compare module/function-edge coverage, audited edge precision, seed counts, per-library supported-pair connectivity, timing and memory. Keep model recovery separate from oracle connectivity.','','The present audit characterizes the unchanged implementation. It does not modify the frozen experiment, fix these defects, claim representative ecosystem coverage, or run models.','','## Reproduction and artifacts','','```sh','node scripts/real-app/multi-bundler/audit/mutations.mjs','python3 scripts/real-app/multi-bundler/audit/run-scale.py','node --test scripts/real-app/multi-bundler/*.test.mjs','python3 scripts/real-app/multi-bundler/audit/report.py','```','','- `mutation-probes.json` includes every exact input fixture, expected/observed relationship and unresolved-import record.','- `mutation-probes.csv` is a compact result table.','- `synthetic-scale.json` records all twelve fresh-process scale cases and counters.','- `existing-tests.txt` confirms the 19 existing tests still pass; the newly exposed gaps are recorded separately.','- `provenance.json` pins unchanged core hashes, Node and esbuild versions.','- `code/` snapshots the exact audit drivers and analyzer components.','']
(out/'REPORT.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines));snapshot=out/'code';snapshot.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
for name in files:shutil.copy2(root/'scripts/real-app/multi-bundler'/name,snapshot/name)
for p in (root/'scripts/real-app/multi-bundler/audit').glob('*'):
 if p.is_file():shutil.copy2(p,snapshot/p.name)
manifest={str(p.relative_to(out)):sha(p)for p in out.rglob('*')if p.is_file()and p.name!='SHA256SUMS.json'};(out/'SHA256SUMS.json').write_text(json.dumps(manifest,indent=2)+'\n');print(json.dumps({'probes':len(rows),'held':sum(r['pass']for r in rows),'gaps':sum(not r['pass']for r in rows),'scaleCases':len(scale),'coreUnchanged':True,'report':str(out/'REPORT.md')}))
